13346:- High — `port/sim/device/check-device-fullgame.sh:1405`, `port/sim/device/riglib.sh:929` — The claim and lease are not session-bound or fenced. The deadman judges every `HOST:*` claim using the prior run’s global sequence value. A new step-0 host can replace an orphaned claim with `HOST:<new nonce>`, yet the old deadman still sees its old frozen lease and deletes the new live claim. Likewise, a suspended host can resume after its claim was reclaimed and execute `rig_daemon_restore` without revalidating ownership. Both actors can then issue the non-idempotent START and leave duplicate daemons requiring manual repair. Fix: bind lease values to the claim nonce, publish/renew liveness for every host claimant, and require an ownership/fencing check immediately before every daemon-plane action. This means Round-6 H2 remains incomplete and meets bar (b).
13348:- High — `port/sim/device/riglib.sh:386`, `port/sim/device/riglib.sh:420`, `port/gfx/check-device-render.sh:911` — Round-7 H2 is not closed for a claimless inherited deadman already inside recovery. The host can scan zero, START, verify one, and remove the marker; the old deadman can then execute its previously-decided START, observe two, and still remove the marker because legacy producers clear it on any nonzero count (`check-device-render.sh:921`). `rig_deadman_quiesce` merely waits for that deadman to exit and performs no post-exit cardinality check. The device proceeds with two instances and no recovery marker. Fix: install a claim-aware successor watchdog before cancelling the inherited one, then quiesce it before normalization, or complete the registered centralized-deadman migration. This meets bar (b).
13352:- High — `port/sim/device/riglib.sh:955` — A deadman holder is not “verifiably dead”: every nonzero result from `dsh "test -d /proc/$pid"` is treated as absence. A transient rc 70/71 followed by transport recovery for the subsequent `rm` deletes a live deadman’s claim, again permitting concurrent START operations. `rig_deadman_quiesce` repeats the rc conflation at line 1003. Fix: capture the rc and permit takeover only on definite device rc 1; transport, malformed-marker, and other statuses must retain the claim and fail closed. This meets bar (b).
13356:- Low — `port/sim/device/riglib.sh:933` — Claim publication reports `CLAIMED` even if writing `owner` fails; deadman publication at `check-device-fullgame.sh:1395` does the same. PID-only `DM:` identity also wedges on PID reuse, and the standdown releases at lines 1435/1443 are not ownership-checked. Fix: status-check publication, use canonical owner grammars with process-generation identity, and route every release through one atomic ownership primitive. These are cap-disposition hardening cases.
